# Password Strength Audit

This is a beginner-friendly cybersecurity project.  
It checks how strong or weak passwords are by comparing their hashes.  
The tool uses the CrackStation wordlist for analysis.  
It shows how easily weak passwords can be cracked.  
This highlights the real-world risk of using simple passwords.  
Strong passwords help prevent account takeovers and data breaches.  

---

# How Passwords Are Stored

Systems store password hashes instead of plain text passwords.  
Hashes are one-way encrypted versions of passwords.  
In Linux, hashes are stored in `/etc/shadow`.  
In Windows, password hashes are stored in the SAM file (`C:\Windows\System32\config\SAM`).  
These files are protected and require admin or root access to view.  
You can also get hashes in CTFs or breach simulation files like `hashes.txt`.  
Once you have a hash, tools like CrackStation can try to crack weak ones.

